WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.779
DC Universe's Titans has provided plenty of
Easter eggs for fans to find.

00:00:05.779 --> 00:00:10.860
There are callbacks to old classics, set pieces
in iconic comic book locations, and a whole

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.130
lot more.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:15.960
Here are the Easter eggs you might have missed
in Titans Season 2.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:21.760
Fairly early in the episode "Aqualad," we
see Arthur Light, a.k.a. Doctor Light, torturing

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:22.920
Dr. Jacob Finlay.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.630
For starters, their conversation indicates
the two have a history.

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:31.680
"I love this gladiator look, reminds me of
the time you went Spartacus on professor Tsu

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.629
for failing the freshman physics final."

00:00:33.629 --> 00:00:37.620
We don't hear a lot of specifics, but the
gist of it seems to be that Finlay has won

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.520
respect and fame on Doctor Light's shoulders.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:45.579
They make reference to failed work Light did
that Finlay was able to improve upon, and

00:00:45.579 --> 00:00:48.629
subsequently take all the credit for.

00:00:48.629 --> 00:00:52.780
But that's just the tip of the iceberg regarding
the relationship between these two.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:53.780
Let's back up.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.160
The name Jacob Finlay comes directly from
the comics.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:03.239
When we first meet Finlay in 1989's Secret
Origins #37, he appears as an apparition to

00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:05.530
Arthur Light in a Doctor Light costume.

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:10.920
A flashback unfolds in which we learn Finlay
was the original Doctor Light before Arthur

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.740
Light took up the name.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:17.010
And unlike Arthur Light, Finlay wanted to
use his abilities for good.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:21.750
After Finlay dies, Arthur takes his suit and
his powers, but continues to be haunted by

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.479
Finlay's ghost.

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:27.570
When Doctor Light tortures Jacob Finlay, a
blink-and-you'll-miss-it Easter egg pays tribute

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:32.890
not to a comic book or another DC-inspired
TV show or movie, but to one of the Titans'

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:33.890
crew.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:38.250
When Doctor Light lays a copy of a magazine
in front of Finlay, Finlay's face is on the

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:42.000
cover, and the story on him was written by
one James Usas.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.609
Well, James Usas isn't a D-list supervillain
or a well-kept comic book secret -- he's no

00:01:47.609 --> 00:01:49.640
Ding Dong Daddy, in other words.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:55.399
Instead, James Anthony Usas is the set designer
for every episode of Titans' second season.

00:01:55.399 --> 00:01:59.999
In the last few years, Usas has also made
a name for himself as a set designer on series

00:01:59.999 --> 00:02:03.359
like The Expanse and Star Trek: Discovery.

00:02:03.359 --> 00:02:09.170
Much of the episode "Aqualad" revolves around
the doomed Garth trying to woo Donna Troy.

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:13.750
In one of his conversations with Donna, Garth
teases her with a joke about her tongue twister

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:15.770
of a homeland, Themyscira.

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:17.920
"Ah, to the Mascara.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.940
It's Themyscira, and yes."

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:27.690
This is possibly a reference to a line of
makeup that most fans have probably forgotten

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:28.690
about.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:34.300
In 2011, MAC Cosmetics released a Wonder Woman-themed
line of makeup products, complete with Wonder

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:37.360
Woman artwork by renowned artist Michael Allred.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.860
The line included such products as Obey Me
nail polish, Athena's Kiss lip gloss and,

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.700
yes, Themyscira Mascara.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:50.020
Toward the beginning of the fifth episode
of season two, Gar goes into Rachel's bedroom

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:56.520
and finds a weird, oily black substance flowing
out of and around Raven as she sleeps.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:01.660
Eventually it cuts Gar, and later in the episode
Rachel shows fresh cuts to Kori on her own

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:03.760
back from the nightly events.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:07.620
We don't know what the exact nature of this
energy is yet, though it seems obvious it

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:12.410
has something to do with how Trigon altered
Rachel's powers in the season premiere.

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:16.670
As Gar enters the room, there's a shot of
something that should probably be raising

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:20.540
some alarm bells, though we have yet to hear
anyone mentioning it.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:25.680
Posted on a cork board above Rachel's desk
are a number of images that wouldn't necessarily

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.810
be disturbing... if they weren't being drawn
by the daughter of a demon.

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:34.611
In fact, they're exactly what you'd expect
any given evil-demonic-possessed kid to draw

00:03:34.611 --> 00:03:38.170
before they go full Brightburn on everyone
around them.

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:43.650
Rachel's creative side seems to be very interested
in black, abyssal holes, which is likely connected

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:47.960
with the darkness continuing to emerge from
Rachel herself.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:52.120
Fans have been waiting for the return of Superboy
and Krypto ever since the first season's finale

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:56.480
gave us a post-credits scene showing the pair
busting out of a Cadmus laboratory.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:01.070
They finally got their wish with the second
season's sixth episode, "Conner."

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:05.440
After revisiting that post-credits scene and
giving us a corny commercial for Cadmus, Superboy

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:10.010
and Krypto escape from the lab in the cold
open of "Conner" and find themselves faced

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:13.300
with the skyline of the famous city of Metropolis.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:18.299
Thankfully, the show's creative team don't
forget an important detail — Metropolis'

00:04:18.299 --> 00:04:21.410
most well-known landmark, the Daily Planet
building.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:26.000
The workplace of Clark Kent, the Daily Planet's
most recognizable physical feature is the

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.230
massive ringed globe on top of the building.

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:32.040
The Titans crew does a wonderful job with
the detail.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:36.700
You wouldn't notice it if you weren't looking
for it, but once you do, there's no mistaking

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:38.340
what it is.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:43.550
After his escape, Conner walks around Metropolis
with Krypto, taking everything in and seeming

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:45.100
to revel in it.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:50.560
At one point he stops and raises his arms,
taking in the rays of the sun, visibly overjoyed

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:54.000
to be among humanity rather than locked away
in a lab.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.460
A nearby street vendor is selling shirts,
caps, and other Metropolis memorabilia including

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:01.670
a black shirt bearing the Superman logo.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:07.990
The park behind the street vendors is Centennial
Park, the fictional Metropolis' answer to

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:09.880
New York's Central Park.

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:15.200
In DC Comics, Centennial Park is the home
of the memorial raised to honor Superman after

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:18.780
he dies in battle with the rampaging villain
Doomsday.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:23.950
It's a fitting location for Superboy to be
as he experiences his own form of rebirth.

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:31.340
"Everything he's experiencing is for the very
first time."

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:36.770
In "Fallen," Mercy Graves and her commandos
raid Titans Tower, recapturing Conner and

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:37.770
Krypto.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:42.590
Seeing Gar's transformation powers in action,
Mercy decides to bring Gar along for good

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:43.590
measure.

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:48.680
The episode ends with Gar in a lounge-like
prison, watching the 1939 classic The Wizard

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:49.889
of Oz.

00:05:49.889 --> 00:05:52.860
It turns out that there's some meaning to
the choice.

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:57.340
The scene Gar is watching brings the Wicked
Witch's flying monkeys into view, and once

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:01.800
you see them it's difficult to not think of
the moment earlier in the episode when Rachel's

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:06.810
powers summon a winged gargoyle who hunts
down her new friend's abusive father.

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:11.970
In both this episode and the following episode,
there are references and reminders of Dorothy's

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:12.970
trip to Oz.

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:18.660
While speaking to the phantom Bruce Wayne,
Dick refers to Deathstroke's "tiny tin heart"

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:21.630
a reference to Oz's Tin Man, who has no heart.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:26.160
And considering the brain surgery forced on
Gar in that same episode, you could see him

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.169
singing "If I Only Had A Brain."

00:06:29.169 --> 00:06:34.850
The two Luthors mentioned the most in "Conner"
are the infamous Lex Luthor and his father,

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:35.850
Lionel.

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:40.290
But we briefly see at least one more member
of the family: Lena Luthor.

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:44.330
Unlike her brother, she's actually smiling
in the family portrait that Conner finds in

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:48.990
the Luthor home, and she's looking lovingly
at the same man we know beat Lex after he'd

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:50.790
had too much to drink.

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:54.260
Lena Luthor has kind of a crazy history in
DC Comics.

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:58.610
Depending on which comic book you read and
when it was published, Lena Luthor is either

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:01.740
Lex Luthor's sister or she's his daughter.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:06.160
She's risen to prominence more recently, moreso
on the small screen than in the comics.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:11.510
Katie McGrath plays Lena Luthor in CW's Supergirl
series, and became a regular in the third

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:12.520
season.

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:16.110
Most episodes of Titans take their titles
from the name of the character who will be

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:18.840
the focus of that particular hour.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:23.430
Season 2's seventh episode, "Bruce Wayne,"
is a departure in that while Bruce Wayne shows

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:27.220
up quite a bit, it isn't really Bruce Wayne.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:31.520
When Dick Grayson's mentor appears to him,
it becomes clear that this isn't the real

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.690
Wayne, but Dick Grayson's insecurities haunting
him in the form of his surrogate father.

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:41.200
One such scene is when Dick goes to a club
looking for information on Deathstroke, and

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:42.390
Wayne appears on stage.

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:48.780
In an unexpected turn of events, he gives
us a tribute to the late Adam West.

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:52.860
"Hold tight.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:56.940
Bat turn."

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:01.901
Iain Glen's dance is brief, but long-time
Batman fans undoubtedly recognized it as a

00:08:01.901 --> 00:08:06.130
call back to Adam West's campy portrayal of
Batman from the '60s.

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:11.420
In Batman's first episode, Batman finds himself
in a disco with the Riddler's lovely assistant,

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:16.170
pulling the trademark moves Glen's Bruce Wayne
gives us on Titans.

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:19.930
In "Jericho," Slade finally tells his son
the truth about who he is.

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:24.830
The story is told through a series of flashbacks,
all leading up to Jericho meeting his father

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:28.130
alone in a church to finally get the truth.

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:39.990
The meeting, uh...doesn't go well.

00:08:39.990 --> 00:08:44.910
Viewers were no doubt expecting to get some
concrete answers from that meet-up, but sharp-eared

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:49.220
viewers may have gotten a wealth of info even
earlier in the episode.

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:54.019
During the episode's first flashback, you
might have missed a super-quick mention of

00:08:54.019 --> 00:08:55.920
a clandestine group called H.I.V.E .:

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.970
"He was part of a special unit called H.I.V.E.

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:05.240
Try to relax."

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:09.420
H.I.V.E. has been a mainstay in DC Comics
since the early '80s.

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:14.670
When they first appear in the pages of New
Teen Titans, their acronym stood for "Hierarchy

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:17.290
of International Vengeance and Extermination."

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:21.410
The group has a long history of animosity
with the Teen Titans, including close ties

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:25.569
with the group known as the Fearsome Five,
which includes villains we've already seen

00:09:25.569 --> 00:09:28.860
on Titans like Shimmer and Doctor Light.

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:34.070
Season 2's ninth episode, "Atonement," opens
with the Titans disbanding, leaving Gar to

00:09:34.070 --> 00:09:36.579
watch over Connor and Titans Tower.

00:09:36.579 --> 00:09:42.350
The subsequent montage shows Gar going from
optimistic and excited to bored and stir-crazy

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:44.029
in three days.

00:09:44.029 --> 00:09:47.860
Along with everything else, the post-its Gar
leaves in the kitchen reflect his devolving

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:52.779
mental state...through the helpful influence
of a couple of great songs and films.

00:09:52.779 --> 00:09:54.670
On the first day he posts "Fitter.

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:55.670
Happier.

00:09:55.670 --> 00:09:59.260
More productive," a line from Radiohead's
"Fitter Happier."

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:04.660
By day two, his emotions are already sagging
a bit, and he writes "Always look on the brightside

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:10.820
of life," which is the song that concludes
the classic 1979 religious satire Monty Python's

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:12.019
Life of Brian.

00:10:12.019 --> 00:10:16.639
It is a little strange that Gar chooses this
phrase, since the characters singing it aren't

00:10:16.639 --> 00:10:19.860
exactly in an enviable position at the time.

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:25.170
By day three, Gar is officially stir-crazy,
and he lets us know by calling back to the

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:27.660
1980 thriller The Shining.

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:33.790
Rather than any affirmations, Gar writes out
"ALL WORK AND NO PLAY MAKES GAR A DULL BOY"

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:38.309
on multiple post-its and sticks them up in
a row a post-it tribute to the moment in The

00:10:38.309 --> 00:10:44.350
Shining when Wendy discovers what her homicidal
husband has really been up to all winter.

00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:48.540
Dick Grayson's time in prison represents a
major turning point for his character arc,

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:50.970
thanks in part to his three new cellmates.

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:55.160
At first it seems like the three are more
than happy with the idea of killing Dick,

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:59.730
but eventually Grayson learns his cellmates
are deserters from a Corto Maltese cartel

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:04.139
in part because they refused to kill on the
cartel's orders.

00:11:04.139 --> 00:11:08.720
Instead of allowing themselves to be sent
back to Corto Maltese where they'll be killed,

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:10.550
the three plan an escape.

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:14.749
During the 10th episode, we find Luis, the
youngest of the three cellmates, carving an

00:11:14.749 --> 00:11:16.610
image on the wall above his bunk.

00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:21.370
He eventually tells Dick this is "Alazul,"
a guardian spirit of his home village that

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:23.040
protects his people.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:27.199
In spite of Dick's warnings, Luis is convinced
Alazul will help him escape.

00:11:27.199 --> 00:11:30.490
"A bird that comes down in the night and saves
people?

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:31.760
Yes.

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:34.779
He's gonna save us tonight, and we'll be free."

00:11:34.779 --> 00:11:38.910
That conversation is part of the reason Dick
decides to help the inmates near the end of

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:42.929
the episode, literally coming out of the night
to save them.

00:11:42.929 --> 00:11:46.290
And sure, the carving on the wall doesn't
look much like the costume Dick eventually

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:50.059
wears as Nightwing, either here or in the
comics.

00:11:50.059 --> 00:11:54.369
But while Titans goes out of its way to make
it clear that this dude is all about some

00:11:54.369 --> 00:11:59.420
wings, viewers may have missed the subtle
meaning of that monochromatic wall carving:

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:04.699
the word comes from "ala azul," which is Spanish
for "blue wing."

00:12:04.699 --> 00:12:07.130
